description:
Write a tool that reads `.csproj` files with `System.Xml.Linq` and reports on them:

```csharp
public record Package(string Name, string Version);
public record ProjectInfo(string? TargetFramework, bool IsExe, bool NullableEnabled, List<Package> Packages, List<string> References);

public static class ProjectReader
{
    public static ProjectInfo Read(string xml)
    public static List<string> Outdated(IEnumerable<ProjectInfo> projects, IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> latest)
}
```

- `TargetFramework` is the element's text, or `null` when missing. `IsExe` is true when `OutputType` is `Exe` (ignoring case). `NullableEnabled` is true when `Nullable` is `enable`.
- `Packages` come from `PackageReference` elements (`Include` and `Version` attributes; a missing version is `""`), sorted by name ignoring case.
- `References` are the `ProjectReference` paths reduced to the project name: `..\Budget.Core\Budget.Core.csproj` gives `Budget.Core`. Paths may use `\` or `/`.
- `Outdated` lists, for every package used in any project whose version is lower than the one in `latest`, a line `"<name>: <version> -> <latest>"`, once per name and version, sorted. Compare versions as numbers part by part (`System.Version`), so `9.0.0` is lower than `10.0.0`. Packages not in `latest`, or with a version that cannot be parsed, are skipped.
- Invalid XML throws `FormatException` with the `XmlException` as its inner exception.

